WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.919 --> 00:00:05.870
Ok, Fermium, it has absolutely no use as far
as anyone knows except

00:00:05.870 --> 00:00:10.970
that it’s extremely radioactive. It’s
named after Enrico Fermi who is a

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:11.970
well-known physicist.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:17.699
Fermi was an Italian physicist who in the
1930s was the first person to

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:24.590
discover artificial radioactivity. This is
where neutrons, which had only

00:00:24.590 --> 00:00:28.689
recently been discovered when they did these
experiments, hit an

00:00:28.689 --> 00:00:33.539
element that is not radioactive, increase
the weight of the nucleus but

00:00:33.539 --> 00:00:38.510
not the number of protons and cause it to
fall apart by radioactive

00:00:38.510 --> 00:00:39.510
decay.

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:44.620
And it also, like Einsteinium, where it was
first found in the debris of a

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:48.789
nuclear bomb, in fact the very first nuclear
bomb that was ever tested.

00:00:48.789 --> 00:00:56.500
Shortly after the fascists took power in Italy,
Fermi moved to the United

00:00:56.500 --> 00:01:02.709
States and, as part of the Manhattan project
for building the atomic

00:01:02.709 --> 00:01:09.530
bomb, he built the first nuclear reactor under
the football stadium, in the

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:13.509
squash courts under the football stadium at
Chicago University, the

00:01:13.509 --> 00:01:18.030
University of Chicago, right in the centre
of Chicago, and they filled the

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.810
squash courts with blocks of carbon with holes
in the middle through

00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:27.859
which they put rods of uranium. So the first
ever sustained nuclear

00:01:27.859 --> 00:01:33.490
reaction took place in the sports field of
University of Chicago and if you

00:01:33.490 --> 00:01:38.149
go now there, the stadium itself has been
completely demolished, but

00:01:38.149 --> 00:01:41.000
there’s a rather fine monument to the birth
of the atomic era.

